  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This scholarly book is showing an early but meaningful uptake in the public administration literature. It has been downloaded 7,100 times through OAPEN, and it has already been cited 8 times, with citations appearing across 2019 to 2025 in journals and edited volumes focused on public policy, administrative reform, and governance.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">European Perspectives for Public Administration has achieved its strongest visible impact through broad open access use and a small but growing scholarly citation record. The download total indicates sustained reading and consultation, while the citation pattern suggests that the book is being drawn into ongoing debates about public administration, public sector reform, and the future of governance in Europe. Because the citation base is still modest, the record is best read as an emerging influence rather than a mature one.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citing works point to a coherent thematic footprint. The book is cited most often in work on public policy and administration research, local government finance and decentralization, and policy transfer and learning. That pattern fits the book\u2019s stated focus on strategies and priorities for the public sector in Europe, and it suggests that readers are using it as a reference point for comparative and forward-looking administrative scholarship rather than as a narrow specialist text.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The reach is also geographically and institutionally diverse within the available record. Citations come from scholars associated with institutions in Belgium and Norway most frequently, alongside contributions linked to Ethiopia, France, Hungary, India, Lithuania, and Russia. The citing venues include journals and book chapters in public administration and related governance fields, which indicates that the book is circulating within academic communities where European public administration is being studied, taught, and reassessed.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Open access availability appears to be an important part of the book\u2019s visibility. The record shows the work as openly accessible through OAPEN and the DOI landing page, which likely supports the strong download activity. Funding acknowledgements linked to the Alexander von Humboldt Foundation and EPPA are also recorded, pointing to institutional support behind the project. Overall, the evidence suggests a book that is being read widely and is beginning to leave a scholarly trace, with the clearest impact visible in usage and early citation uptake.</p>",
